course: dictionary
lesson: 7003
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Paralysis can mean losing the ability to move part of your body, or being unable to act, decide or work. The words around it show you which meaning is right.
explain: «Polio can cause paralysis; vaccination has made it rare.» Polio is an illness that harms the nerves, so here paralysis means losing the ability to move some or all of the body. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check it still makes sense.
explain: Now read «Ella felt a paralysis of fear and could not decide what to do.» Fear does not stop a body from moving in the same way an illness does. It stops you from acting or deciding, so this is the second meaning.
fact: Paralysis is a noun, so it names a thing or a state.
fact: «Paralysis» means loss of the ability to move some or all of the body, usually because of injury or illness to the nerves.
fact: «Paralysis» can also mean not being able to act, decide or work, as in «traffic paralysis».
fact: Polio can cause paralysis; vaccination has made it rare.
fact: Many people with paralysis use wheelchairs and live full lives.
exercise: «Polio can cause paralysis; vaccination has made it rare.» What does paralysis mean here? | A feeling of great joy | A type of medicine | *Loss of the ability to move some or all of the body | Heavy snow on the roads
exercise: «Snow caused traffic paralysis across the city.» What does paralysis mean here? | A shiny new road | A fast race between cars | *A state where traffic cannot move or work properly | A loud horn sound
exercise: «Ella felt a paralysis of fear and could not decide what to do.» What does paralysis mean here? | Being sure of what to do | *Being unable to act or decide, because of fear | Being very tired after a race | Feeling hungry
test: In «Polio can cause paralysis», the word means: | Great speed | *Loss of movement in part of the body, caused by illness | A state of being unable to decide | A kind of weather
test: Which sentence uses paralysis to mean being unable to act or work? | *The strike caused paralysis of the whole port, and no ships could leave | The patient spoke about his wheelchair | The nerve damage caused paralysis in her left leg | The doctor checked the nerves with a small light
exam: Which word is closest in meaning to paralysis in «a paralysis of fear»? | Eagerness | Laughter | Brightness | *Inability to act
